WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.051
Imagine drawing a normalized database as a
graph. Each row is a node, and each foreign-key

00:00:05.051 --> 00:00:10.158
reference is an edge. Placing rows on storage
machines becomes a partitioning problem: keep

00:00:10.158 --> 00:00:14.031
related rows together while keeping the machines
reasonably balanced.

00:00:14.031 --> 00:00:19.133
An edge crossing a partition boundary suggests a
network lookup during a join. Some graphs

00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:24.234
divide naturally, such as independent accounts
with their own orders. Social relationships

00:00:24.234 --> 00:00:29.110
and shared reference data are harder because
many users connect across any boundary we

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:29.506
choose.

00:00:29.531 --> 00:00:34.310
Even an excellent placement cannot remove every
crossing. Counting rows alone misses the

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:38.764
workload: a frequently followed edge can matter
more than thousands of rarely used

00:00:38.764 --> 00:00:39.361
references.

00:00:39.531 --> 00:00:44.750
Denormalization changes that problem by copying
information into the aggregate that needs

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:50.146
it. A profile document can contain the region
name, employment entries and education entries

00:00:50.146 --> 00:00:55.541
instead of requiring a separate lookup for each
item. Fetching the profile then requires one

00:00:55.541 --> 00:00:59.647
bounded access path, which is easier to
distribute by user identifier.

00:00:59.647 --> 00:01:05.168
The cost appears when shared information
changes. A region rename that once modified one
row

00:01:05.168 --> 00:01:10.569
may now require updates to many profiles, and
intermediate states can disagree. Follow the

00:01:10.569 --> 00:01:15.551
arrows from one shared region to two copied
profiles: duplication removes read-time

00:01:15.551 --> 00:01:18.492
communication but creates write-time
maintenance.

00:01:18.647 --> 00:01:22.266
It moves work and responsibility rather than
making them disappear.

00:01:22.266 --> 00:01:27.556
A local hash table turns a key into a storage
location. A distributed hash table extends

00:01:27.556 --> 00:01:32.726
that idea by assigning ranges of hash values to
different machines. Suppose a teaching

00:01:32.726 --> 00:01:38.076
example uses hashes from zero through ninety-
nine: node A owns zero through thirty-two, B

00:01:38.076 --> 00:01:41.863
owns thirty-three through sixty-five, and C owns
the remainder.

00:01:41.863 --> 00:01:47.062
A client hashes an account key, finds the
containing range in its routing map and contacts

00:01:47.062 --> 00:01:52.146
the responsible node. The same key must produce
the same hash for all clients using that

00:01:52.146 --> 00:01:57.057
configuration. No central dispatcher needs to
execute every data request, which helps

00:01:57.057 --> 00:02:00.928
different clients make progress independently as
the cluster grows.

00:02:00.928 --> 00:02:05.843
Hashing deliberately scatters nearby input keys.
That is useful for distributing many

00:02:05.843 --> 00:02:11.105
distinct keys, but it removes the original key
order. Asking for one known account is easy;

00:02:11.105 --> 00:02:16.252
asking for every account between two identifiers
cannot generally be answered by scanning

00:02:16.252 --> 00:02:17.929
one contiguous hash interval.

00:02:17.929 --> 00:02:23.348
Nor does a uniform distribution of keys imply
uniform traffic: one extremely popular key

00:02:23.348 --> 00:02:28.766
still maps to one replica group. In the diagram,
distinguish the key-to-hash calculation

00:02:28.766 --> 00:02:33.815
from the routing map. The first is deterministic
computation; the second is shared

00:02:33.815 --> 00:02:35.662
configuration that can change.

00:02:35.662 --> 00:02:40.686
A correct hash calculation with an obsolete
ownership map can still send a request to the

00:02:40.686 --> 00:02:41.476
wrong machine.

00:02:41.662 --> 00:02:46.980
A cluster containing many machines should expect
individual failures. If every key has only

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:52.238
one copy, losing a node makes that node's range
unavailable and may lose data permanently.

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:56.504
Replication stores each key on several nodes so
another copy can survive.

00:02:56.662 --> 00:03:01.515
Keep the distinction between cluster size and
replication factor clear: a cluster might

00:03:01.515 --> 00:03:06.534
contain hundreds of machines while each key has
three replicas. A write to all replicas of

00:03:06.534 --> 00:03:10.717
one key then involves three destinations, not
every machine in the cluster.

00:03:10.717 --> 00:03:15.582
This bounded redundancy preserves the basic
scaling idea while improving durability and

00:03:15.582 --> 00:03:20.224
recovery options, provided the replicas do not
all share the same failure boundary.

00:03:20.224 --> 00:03:25.688
Replication introduces a new timing problem.
Initially A, B and C store x equal to one. A

00:03:25.688 --> 00:03:30.784
client writes x equal to two, and A receives the
update before B and C. During this

00:03:30.784 --> 00:03:36.248
interval, different replicas can answer the same
read differently even though no disk has

00:03:36.248 --> 00:03:36.678
failed.

00:03:36.724 --> 00:03:41.851
Network delays, queueing and scheduling make
simultaneous arrival unrealistic. Now imagine

00:03:41.851 --> 00:03:46.979
that the network separates A from the other
copies. If both sides must answer immediately,

00:03:46.979 --> 00:03:51.765
they cannot always know whether an unseen write
has occurred elsewhere. If they must

00:03:51.765 --> 00:03:56.323
preserve a single real-time-consistent history,
some requests must wait or fail.

00:03:56.323 --> 00:04:01.247
The partition makes this choice unavoidable; it
is not solved by drawing more replica

00:04:01.247 --> 00:04:01.652
arrows.
